"""mk_analysts.py : the briefs of the five analysts who count v0.57's bench from the readers' own lines (they never see a picture).
Copies the tallies to vision/isolation_v057/digest/ and writes analyst_1..5.txt there."""
import os, shutil
ROOT = "E:/Cognitive_Test_Scene_Creator/"
D = ROOT + "vision/isolation_v057/digest/"
os.makedirs(D, exist_ok=True)
HEAD = """You are digesting a bench test of cartoon pictures. Blind readers each described pictures in their own words; for every thing under test a file holds each reader's own lines about it. Count what the readers wrote and quote them. You never see the pictures.

How a file is laid out: a reading is headed `== <picture name> arm <X> reader <n> (<pic id>)`, and under it are that reader's lines: `F:` from the free description, `I:` from the inventory, `U:` from the reader's own list of things it was unsure of. The lines were picked by a list of words, so some lines are about other things in the picture: leave those aside. The arms:
- S: the picture as the last release drew it, in the reading that release's own screen had of it (headed `arm S (v0.56's screen, <pic id>)`).
- O: the same picture as the last release drew it, read again by a fresh reader.
- B: the same picture built again with the new drawing.
- C: the same picture with an alternative new drawing (said per row below).
- P: (the pan only) the same picture with the drawing of the release before last.
- F: a new picture built with the new drawing; it has no old counterpart.
- G: the same new picture as F with the old drawing.

Read only the files named below, each with the Read tool; do not list or search folders. For each row write one file with the Write tool, {D}digest_<row>.md, holding:
1. A table with one line per arm and the counts the row asks for. Count readings, not lines. A reading whose lines do not let you decide is counted as `cannot tell`. Each count's readings must add up to the arm's number of readings.
2. Under the table, for every reading you did not count under the row's first heading (a): one line with the picture name, the arm, the pic id and the reader's own words, quoted exactly and whole on one line.
3. Three to five sentences on what differs between the arms, with the counts. Say nothing the counts do not carry, and do not say which drawing should be kept.

When all your files are written, reply with the tables only, one per row, each under its row's name.

The rows:
""".replace("{D}", D)
ROWS = {
 "pan": """ROW pan (file {D}tally_pan.txt). The thing: a person waters flowers with a frying pan instead of a watering can, which is the wrong tool. Arms S and O: a shallow dark pan on a thin handle, the water leaving its far tip in an arc. Arm P: a deep dark pan. Arm B: a hollow pan with a pale metal inside on a dark wooden handle, tipped, the water running over its rim in a broad sheet. Arm C: as B with a dark inside. Count per arm: (a) readings that call the thing a frying pan, a pan or a skillet plainly in the free description or the inventory, with no other name for it anywhere; (b) readings that call it a frying pan or pan with another name beside it anywhere (say which: watering can, pot, saucepan, ladle, bowl, scoop, shovel), or that give the pan only in the unsure section; (c) readings that call it something else first or only (quote the name). Then, in a second table, per arm: how many readings say that a pan is the wrong or an odd thing to water with (in any words), and how many do not remark on it. Then list, one line a reading, the first word the reading uses for the vessel.""",
 "seesaw_heavy": """ROW seesaw_heavy (file {D}tally_seesaw_heavy.txt). The thing: a grown man sits on the low end of a seesaw and a child sits stuck up in the air on the other end; what a reader should infer is that he is too heavy for her, so she cannot get down. Arms S and O: the plank's ends rest on small dark blocks on the grass, and the man reads a book or a newspaper. Arm B: each end of the plank carries a yellow seat with a back, with rubber buffers under the plank's ends, and the man reads. Arm C: as B, but the man has no book: he sits with a hand on each knee and looks up at the child. Count per arm: (a) readings that give his weight as why she is up or stuck (he is heavier, too heavy, his weight keeps her up, she is too light), plainly; (b) readings that give his weight beside another reading, or only in the unsure section; (c) readings that say she is stuck up or cannot get down and give no weight (quote what they give: he ignores her, does not notice, just sits); (d) readings with neither (two people on a seesaw, playing). Then, in a second table, per arm: (e) readings that doubt where or how the man sits (on the grass, perched, forward of the seat, his feet on the ground as odd), each quoted; (f) readings that remark on the blocks, the buffers or the seats as odd or unclear, each quoted. For arm C list what each reading says the man is doing.""",
 "catch": """ROW catch (file {D}tally_catch.txt). The thing: one figure throws a ball to another, who holds out both hands to catch it. Arms S and O: the ball flies in a high arc and stands well above the catcher's hands, in two of the three pictures at the height of the garden fence's top. Arm B: the same three pictures with a flat flight toward the catcher's hands, kept off the fence's top line. Arm F: five new pictures with the flat flight. Arm G: the same five new pictures with the old high arc. Count per arm: (a) readings that say plainly who throws and who catches (a game of catch; X throws the ball to Y, who is ready to catch it) with no doubt anywhere about the ball's path, about who throws or about what the catcher does; (b) readings that say it but doubt the ball's path or aim in any section (too high, over the head, over the fence, at the cat, away from the catcher, which way it goes), each quoted; (c) readings that doubt who throws or who catches, or what the catcher is doing (reaching, waving, clapping), each quoted; (d) other. Then list every reading that names a fence, a cat or a pond in connection with the ball, quoted.""",
 "kite": """ROW kite (file {D}tally_kite.txt). The thing: a kite is caught in a tree and a child holds its reel; what a reader should infer is that the kite got stuck. Arms S and O: the string hangs in one sagging curve from the branch to the child's raised hand. Arm B: the string drops from the reel to the grass, lies there in a curl and runs along the ground before it rises to the branch. Count per arm: (a) readings that say the kite is stuck or caught in the tree, plainly, with no other reading of it anywhere; (b) readings that say stuck with flying (in front of the tree, near it, low) or another reading offered beside it anywhere, each quoted; (c) readings that say flying first or only; (d) other. Then list every remark on the string (slack, on the ground, tangled, long, odd), quoted, by arm.""",
 "pond": """ROW pond (file {D}tally_pond.txt). The thing: a figure stands in front of a pond and throws bread to ducks, or fishes with a rod. Arms S and O: the figure stood where the pond's near shore crossed its legs or hips. Arm B: the figure stands lower on the lawn, and the shore passes behind its shoulders or head. Count per arm: (a) readings with no doubt or remark about where the figure stands; (b) readings with any doubt or remark about where the figure stands relative to the pond (on the rim, in the water, overlapping the edge, wading, at the edge as odd), each quoted. Then, per arm: is the action read plainly (feeding the ducks, fishing)? Count, and quote any reading in which it is not.""",
 "wind": """ROW wind (file {D}tally_wind.txt). The thing: the wind has blown a cap off a person's head, the person reaches up after it, and curved lines show the wind. Pictures T3_park_s23711 and T3_park_s23728: in arms S and O the cap and the wind's lines are over a pond; in arm B the person stands elsewhere and the cap and the lines are over hills and sky. Picture T3_garden_s23051: washing on a line is blown by the same wind; in arm B the washing hangs straight from its pegs and curves out toward its hem. Count per arm: (a) readings that say plainly that the wind blew the cap or hat off; (b) readings that say it with another reading beside it (thrown, waving, catching), each quoted; (c) other. Then list, by arm, every remark on the wind's lines (taken for ripples or water, on the wrong side of the cap, odd, the direction doubted) and every remark on where the cap is, quoted. For the garden picture list, by arm, what each reading says of the washing.""",
 "grey": """ROW grey (file {D}tally_grey.txt). The thing: colours were changed. Pictures T3_garden_s23015 and T3_garden_s26007: a fence is being painted and a dog has walked through the paint and left prints; in arms S and O the paint is a light pink, in arm B a deeper pink. Pictures T3_garden_s23020 and T3_garden_s26007: a person who walks while reading is about to trip over a box or a bucket; in arm B the box of T3_garden_s23020 is a darker wood. Count per arm: (a) for the two paint pictures, the colour word each reading gives the paint (list one line a reading), and how many readings name the painted part of the fence, the prints and the dog as the one who made them; (b) for the two tripping pictures, what the thing in the walker's way is called, and how many readings say plainly that the walker is about to trip over it, how many say it beside another reading (quoted), and how many do not say it. Then list any remark, in any picture, on a colour that is hard to see or on a thing that is hard to make out, quoted.""",
}
GROUPS = [["pan"], ["seesaw_heavy"], ["catch"], ["kite", "pond"], ["wind", "grey"]]
for row in ROWS:
    shutil.copyfile(ROOT + "tpdb_work/v057/bench/tally_%s.txt" % row, D + "tally_%s.txt" % row)
for i, g in enumerate(GROUPS, 1):
    open(D + "analyst_%d.txt" % i, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n").write(HEAD + "\n" + "\n\n".join(ROWS[r].replace("{D}", D) for r in g) + "\n")
    print("analyst", i, g)
